@Mike

When I tried my first sauna, I said:"Not bad. It's only dry heat."

When I started to leave, they said: "Go jump in the lake!"

@Sam Lyons

At least you went with the right kind of felt!

I was so far off, but that's the one that put the biggest smile on my face when I finally got it.

@Vaer

Brilliant connecting of the dots of the six degrees of separation!

I wonder if he did give a speech, though. A good friend of mine worked with him in the recording studio as the producer's assistant and said he was quite shy, introverted, and deep.

@HeathieJ

Really? It's not like I find the process enjoyable or anything, but the instructions that come with Ikea furniture are always very clear and following them is easy. I have never understood people describing putting together Ikea products as some sort of ordeal.

(That being said, low quality of Ikea furniture means I haven't bought anything from them in a decade, since we got our own condo - I used to furnish our rental apartments at Ikea).

@Andrzej

I can see why that’s the case. If this were a puzzle in Spanish or French (even if I spoke either with a fluency on par with yours in English, which I don’t), I would struggle mightily. The bulk of the puzzle sits somewhere midway between the American vernacular and slang. 

I have colleagues and friends here in Europe whose English is flawless. We talk, we text, we joke around. Still, I don’t think I ever have used or would use expressions like SHOOKETH with them (mostly because it’s good to preserve one’s dignity…)  But I’m sure I’ve said that and much worse to my friends back home, the ones who knew me in junior high.

